f the adorable Trinity.","verses":246,"father":"","mother":"","v":[14]},{"id":"hermogenes_1503","name":"Hermogenes","also":"","summary":"Mercury-born, at one time Paul’s fellow-labourer in Asia Minor, who, however, afterwards abandoned him, along with one Phygellus, probably on account of the perils by which they were beset (2 Tim. 1:15).","verses":1,"father":"","mother":"","v":[15]},{"id":"phygellus_2356","name":"Phygellus","also":"","summary":"Fugitive, a Christian of Asia, who “turned away” from Paul during his second imprisonment at Rome (2 Tim. 1:15).","verses":1,"father":"","mother":"","v":[15]},{"id":"onesiphorus_2252","name":"Onesiphorus","also":"","summary":"Bringing profit, an Ephesian Christian who showed great kindness to Paul at Rome. He served him in many things, and had oft refreshed him.","verses":2,"father":"","mother":"","v":[16]}],"places":[{"id":"asia_108","name":"Asia","kind":"Region","lat":39.64015,"lon":31.03325,"summary":"Is used to denote Proconsular Asia, a Roman province which embraced the western parts of Asia Minor, and of which Ephesus was the capital, in Acts 2:9; 6:9; 16:6; 19:10, 22; 20:4, 16, 18, etc., and probably Asia Minor in Acts 19:26, 27; 21:27; 24:18; 27:2.","verses":18,"v":[15]},{"id":"rome_1013","name":"Rome","kind":"City","lat":41.89262,"lon":12.48435,"summary":"The most celebrated city in the world at the time of Christ. It is said to have been founded B.C. 753. When the New Testament was written, Rome was enriched and adorned with the spoils of the world, and contained a population estimated at 1,200,000, of which the half were slaves, and including representatives of nearly every nation then known.","verses":9,"v":[17]},{"id":"ephesus_400","name":"Ephesus","kind":"City","lat":37.94194,"lon":27.33972,"summary":"The capital of proconsular Asia, which was the western part of Asia Minor. It was colonized principally from Athens. In the time of the Romans it bore the title of “the first and greatest metropolis of Asia.”","ve